Microsoft Learn: Configure Azure Files and Azure File Sync

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Azure Files and Azure File Sync
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure and manage Azure Files and Azure File Sync to provide centralized, cloud-based file storage accessible across multiple geographic locations.

This module focused on implementing Azure file shares, comparing file shares with blob storage, configuring data protection features, and using Azure File Sync to improve access to organizational documents across distributed offices.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Files

The module introduced Azure Files as a fully managed cloud file-sharing service.

Azure Files allows organizations to create shared file storage accessible through industry-standard protocols such as SMB (Server Message Block).

Common Azure Files use cases:
Shared organizational documents
Lift-and-shift file server migrations
Centralized department storage
Hybrid cloud file sharing
Cross-region collaboration

The learning scenario involved a company with offices in multiple geographic regions needing access to the most up-to-date versions of organizational documents.

Step 2: Azure Files vs Blob Storage

The module compared Azure Files with Azure Blob Storage.

Key differences:
Azure Files	Azure Blob Storage
File share storage	Object storage
SMB protocol support	HTTP/HTTPS access
Traditional file system structure	Flat object storage
Ideal for shared file access	Ideal for unstructured data
Important lessons learned:
Azure Files works similarly to traditional network file shares
Blob Storage is optimized for scalable object storage
Azure Files is better for shared user documents and legacy applications
Blob Storage is preferred for media, backups, and large-scale application data

Choosing the correct storage solution depends on workload requirements and access patterns.

